=== Climate ===
The [[w:Climate|climate]] of Harujima ranges from a [[w:Tropical rainforest climate|Tropical Rainforest climate]] (Köppen ''Af'') in the far north to a [[w:Tropical monsoon climate|Tropical Monsoon climate]] (Köppen ''Am'') further south. Meteorological data from the Shinagawa Ward weather station in Okami illustrates a highly consistent temperature range of 25°C to 35°C (77°F to 95°F) year-round. The island receives substantial annual [[w:Precipitation|rainfall]], typically measuring between 2,000 and 3,000 millimeters.

This high level of precipitation, combined with mountainous [[w:Rain shadow|rain shadows]], historically dictated Harujima's agricultural development through ''tanbo'' (wet-rice) cultivation. Introduced around 225 BC, these advanced [[w:Irrigation|irrigation]] techniques revolutionized the northern plains, providing the caloric surplus necessary for the early imperial court to sustain a professional standing army and an urban administrative class.

== History ==
=== Prehistory and Early Settlement ===
[[w:Archaeology|Archaeological]] evidence indicates that Harujima was the site of the first human settlement in the Okaiken archipelago. The Harujima-Kaze culture arrived approximately 10,000 to 20,000 BC, driven southward by post-[[w:Glacial period|glacial]] climate stabilization. These semi-[[w:Nomad|nomadic]] groups thrived as [[w:Hunter-gatherer|hunter-gatherers]] on the abundant marine life of the northern coast before the later arrival of seafaring [[w:Agrarian society|agrarian]] technologies from the continent.

=== Medieval Unification (Soga Era) ===
The medieval history of Harujima is defined by the rise of the ''Bushi'' ([[w:Samurai|warrior]]) class. In 1201, the Soga Clan, under the leadership of Soga Ujiteru, initiated a campaign of total annexation in the north. This sweeping military victory effectively "shattered the Aesthetic Hegemony" of the refined, poetry-focused courts of Mito, shifting power decisively toward maritime military outposts.

This era of martial dominance is immortalized in the ''Tale of the Soga Brothers'', a foundational relic of Sanese martial [[w:Literature|literature]]. In 1208, Ujiteru was granted the title of [[w:Shogun|Shogun]], establishing a military capital on Harujima that balanced the ritualistic authority of the old imperial court.

=== Modern Political Integration ===
During the "Silent Years" of the Republican Era (1968–2011), Harujima served as the testing ground for the "Great Transformation" policies, which rapidly digitized the island's infrastructure. The Jutsu Restoration of 2011 saw a return to traditional state structures, with Okami officially reinstated as the capital’s name. Harujima’s resilience as a center of power was further highlighted by the transition to "Functional Royalty" protocols, which reintegrated the Imperial Family into the scientific, technological, and industrial sectors headquartered on the island.

== Demographics and Urban Centers ==
Harujima is the most densely populated island in Okaiken. The [[w:Demographics|demographic]] makeup is a complex mosaic dominated by the native majority, alongside historically integrated minority groups. The island’s urban youth population is heavily influenced by the National Competency Exam and the STEP REP Program, which mandates bilingual fluency to maintain competitiveness in Harujima’s booming tech corridors.

As of the 2026 national census estimates, Harujima has a total population of approximately 52,450,000. The racial and ethnic composition of the island is predominantly Sanese-Yamato, accounting for 82.4% (43,218,800) of the population. The island also hosts significant minority populations, primarily descended from those who arrived via the nation’s burgeoning maritime trade networks and mercantile colonies during the 15th and 16th centuries, concentrated today in the major industrial corridors. The Zainichi Kwangjuns constitute the largest minority group at 9.1% (4,772,950), followed by the Zainichi Zong at 6.3% (3,304,350). Other ethnic groups and foreign expatriates make up the remaining 2.2% (1,153,900).

